// Knock Out Roses (Potted) — Rosa 'Radrazz' (Double Knock Out)
// Edit this file to update plant-specific content.

#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Knock Out Roses (Potted)", "Rosa 'Radrazz' (Double Knock Out)", "knockout_rose")[
  Knock Out Roses are exceptionally disease-resistant, repeat-blooming landscape roses producing waves of bright red blooms from June through frost. In containers, they are more versatile and moveable but require more attentive irrigation and fertilization than in-ground plants. Located on the east side of the house — morning sun only, with afternoon shade from the house beginning around midday. This is a favorable exposure: afternoon shade blocks the hottest, most desiccating hours (1–5pm), reducing watering demand and stress on container roots. Potted plants use bagged potting mix managed separately from in-ground soil conditions.
]

=== Container Setup

#warning-callout("Pea Gravel Replacement Recommended")[
  If the bottom third of the pots is currently filled with pea gravel, this should be replaced. Gravel layers at pot bottoms do not improve drainage as commonly believed — water perches above the gravel layer until the finer soil above reaches saturation, then drains slowly. Removing gravel actually improves both drainage and pot weight simultaneously. *Recommended replacement:* polystyrene foam packing peanuts (not cornstarch/biodegradable type — these decompose). Fill the bottom third with peanuts, cover with a layer of fine mesh or landscape fabric to prevent potting mix from filtering down, then add potting mix on top. Near-weightless and non-decomposing. Potential weight reduction: 15–20 lbs or more per large pot.
]

*Potting mix:* Miracle-Gro Moisture Control (peat/coir base, initial pH 5.5–6.5). The slow-release fertilizer charge depletes after one season. Refresh the top third of the mix each spring with fresh Miracle-Gro Moisture Control.

*pH drift:* After a season of Commerce City's alkaline irrigation water, container mix pH drifts from its initial 5.5–6.5 toward 6.5–7.2. At the spring refresh, work a small amount of elemental sulfur granules into the new top mix — container volume is small and peat has low buffering capacity, so this intervention is actually effective here, unlike in-ground applications to clay soil.

*Moisture retention improvements (do at spring refresh):*
- Work hydrogel crystals (Soil Moist or similar polyacrylamide product) into the mix — extends time between waterings
- Add a 1–2 inch layer of fine mulch (cocoa hull or finely shredded bark) on the soil surface — reduces surface evaporation and moderates soil temperature. *Note: cocoa hull is toxic to dogs if ingested in quantity.*
- White Bacopa planted in the same container shades the soil surface, further reducing evaporation

=== Watering

Check soil moisture by pressing a finger 2 inches into the soil — water when it feels dry at that depth. The east-facing, afternoon-shaded exposure means daily watering is not always necessary, unlike full-sun container roses. Water thoroughly until it runs freely from drainage holes. Do NOT allow pots to sit in standing water. Reduce watering in September–October as temperatures drop. Continue watering until pots are moved to winter storage or until just before a hard freeze.

=== Fertilization

Container roses require more frequent fertilization than in-ground plants since nutrients leach out with every watering. Your tested garden soil conditions do NOT apply here — the bagged potting mix starts relatively neutral. Use a rose fertilizer that is relatively low in phosphorus with higher potassium — formulas like 5-1-7 or specialty rose feeds labeled for repeat bloom. Avoid standard high-P bloom boosters. Feed every 3–4 weeks from April through mid-August with a liquid rose fertilizer. Stop feeding 6 weeks before expected first frost (approximately mid-August for Commerce City). Include a liquid chelated iron (EDTA form, not EDDHA) as a monthly drench from May through August — as irrigation water gradually raises container pH through summer, EDTA chelated iron maintains iron availability in the mix.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Knock Out Roses are self-cleaning — they do not require deadheading to rebloom, though removing spent flowers tidies appearance. In early spring (late March–April), cut the entire plant back by one-third to one-half to promote vigorous new growth and strong summer bloom. Remove any dead or crossing canes at this time. Remove rose hips in fall for a cleaner winter appearance.

=== Wildlife Value

Knock Out Roses are excellent for pollinators — the semi-double flowers provide accessible nectar for native bees, bumble bees, and butterflies throughout the long bloom season. Hummingbirds occasionally probe the open flowers. House finches may eat rose hips in fall if left on the plant. Cottontail rabbits will actively browse potted roses placed at ground level — elevate pots on stands to prevent rabbit browsing damage. The pot environment limits rabbit access better than in-ground planting.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=rose+black+spot+treatment")[Black spot] (circular black spots on leaves causing defoliation) is the most common rose disease — Knock Out is very resistant but not immune. Treat with fungicide containing myclobutanil or tebuconazole at the first sign. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=rose+spider+mites+treatment")[Spider mites] are especially problematic on container roses in hot, dry weather — look for stippling and fine webbing; treat with horticultural oil or miticide. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=rose+aphids+treatment")[Aphids] cluster on new growth in spring — dislodge with a strong water spray or treat with insecticidal soap. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+beetles+roses+treatment")[Japanese beetles] (bronze-green beetles skeletonizing leaves) are an increasing Front Range pest — handpick in the morning, or use neem oil; avoid Japanese beetle traps which attract more beetles than they catch.

=== Other Care

WINTER PROTECTION: Container Knock Out Roses in Zone 5b need winter protection — the root system in an unprotected pot can freeze solid, which kills the plant even if the canes are cold-hardy. Options in order of effectiveness: (1) Move the pot into an unheated garage or shed before a hard freeze (below 20°F) — keep barely moist through winter, do not let soil completely freeze or dry out. This is the most reliable option. (2) If leaving pots outdoors: group pots together against the east-facing house wall — provides wind shelter from prevailing north/northwest winter winds and thermal buffering. Wrap the *pot bodies* (not the plants) in multiple layers of burlap, bubble wrap, or foam pipe insulation — more layers meaningfully better than one. Install a frost cloth or burlap tent over the entire plant and pot together to trap ground heat and protect canes from desiccating winter wind. Remove coverings on warm days above 40°F to prevent overheating and mold; replace before evening. Honest caveat: this reduces risk substantially but does not eliminate it in a bad Zone 5b winter.

Repot every 2–3 years into a slightly larger container with fresh potting mix. Ensure pots have adequate drainage holes.
